{
  "an2.1:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 2.1–10 ",
  "an2.1:0.2": "The Chapter on Punishments ",
  "an2.1:1.0": "1. Faults ",
  "an2.1:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "an2.1:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "an2.1:1.3": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, ",
  "an2.1:1.4": "“Mendicants!” ",
  "an2.1:1.5": "“Venerable sir,” they replied. ",
  "an2.1:1.6": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "an2.1:2.1": "“There are, mendicants, these two faults. ",
  "an2.1:2.2": "What two? ",
  "an2.1:2.3": "The fault apparent in this life, and the fault to do with lives to come. ",
  "an2.1:2.4": "What is the fault apparent in this life? ",
  "an2.1:2.5": "It’s when someone sees that kings have arrested a bandit, a criminal, and subjected them to various punishments—",
  "an2.1:2.6": "whipping, caning, and clubbing; cutting off hands or feet, or both; cutting off ears or nose, or both; the ‘porridge pot’, the ‘shell-shave’, the ‘Rāhu’s mouth’, the ‘garland of fire’, the ‘burning hand’, the ‘bulrush twist’, the ‘bark dress’, the ‘antelope’, the ‘meat hook’, the ‘coins’, the ‘caustic pickle’, the ‘twisting bar’, the ‘straw mat’; being splashed with hot oil, being fed to the hounds, being impaled alive, and being beheaded. ",
  "an2.1:3.1": "It occurs to them: ",
  "an2.1:3.2": "‘If I were to commit the kinds of bad deeds for which the kings arrested that bandit, that criminal, ",
 